Z||000000||KnightRyder31||09-12-2000||10:49 AM||||When I'm driving my fiero 70+ MPH, my whole car shakes very hard...not just the steering wheel, but the whole car. What do you guys think? Front end allignment? Balence tires? Ball Joint? Rack and Pinion? I'm not sure.||||reg||1
Z||000001||fierocrazy||09-12-2000||10:54 AM||||i would try front shocks first, maybe you lost a wheel weight?||||reg||1
Z||000002||KnightRyder31||09-12-2000||10:56 AM||||How do I test it? Oh by the way...I just put diff. set of wheels and tires on the car...both used.||||reg||1
Z||000003||HugGRRRFiero||09-12-2000||11:00 AM||||I'd have the used tires balanced.  If that's the only thing you've changed, then I would say the problem likely has something to do with those.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/huggrrr.gif">   <BR>   <A HREF="mailto:HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com">HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||09-12-2000||11:19 AM||||If you are using used tires/wheels, They need to be on the same side of the car as the one they came off of. I.E. If the tire was on the left side you need to make sure it's on your left side. Radial tires get use to the side they are mounted and if you switch sides it causes problems.||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||09-12-2000||11:37 AM||||I'd reballance the tires....<P>Orief, I've heard that keep radials on the same side for years and wondered if it was true. It's usually applied to tire rotation. So awhile back I researched it.<P>What I found is that the whole thing appears to be related to Unidirectional treads. These tires must always be mounted to rotate in the direction of the arrow on the sidewall.<P>Somewhere along the line this was falsely expanded to all radials. (There are no Bias tires with Unidirectional tread. At least none most shops ever saw.) Testing by multiple tire makers has shown no tendancy for normal Radials to care about direction of rotation and they even recomend traditional cross rotaion of the tires on cars with 4 tires the same size. (There are notes about this on a couple of the tire web sites. I've lost the links to the specific sites.)<P>My own experience backs this up. I've mounted enough used radials that I lost count many years ago. I've never once had a problem caused by mounting a good tire on the "wrong" side. I cross rotate all my own radials. <P>With used tires I'd worry most about tread wear, seperated belts, and ballance. Getting a good ballance on used rubber can be a real pain. <P>If the used tire is already mounted when you get it you have to search for weights really carefully and make sure all the brakedust and mud that builds up in the back are removed. Especially on alumnium wheels. I've found stick on weights hidden in the dirt more than once. If you ballance the tire with this crap in there you'll never get them right or keep them that way if you do.||||reg||1
Z||000006||KnightRyder31||09-12-2000||11:39 AM||||DAMN!!! How am I supost to remember that???!!! LOL. I'll just get them balanced today...and see what happens. Hows that sound?||||reg||1
Z||000007||mbramble||09-12-2000||11:46 AM||||My '87 Coupe used to feel like it was going to shake to pieces at 62-67 MPH.<P>I just recently put on new tires, on some old turbo wheels I bought and refinished, had them balanced and had a 4 wheel alignment done.  The tech told me that the rear wheels were really out of alignment.<P>Since then, there is no longer a shake at any speed -- well, at least up to the 85 I've had it to since the work was done.  <P>||||reg||1
